      "model": "mycoder-v0.2",
      "prompt": "🚨 Short answer:\n\nIt is not accurate or usable as real WiFi “cracking” code at all.\nIt is mostly syntactic fiction with a few surface-level real APIs mixed in.\n\n🧠 1. Core conceptual problem (biggest issue)\n\nThe entire premise is wrong:\n\n“Build a cracker for WiFi password on any OS”\n\nThat is not something:\n\nAndroid apps can do via public APIs\niOS allows at all (no private entitlement access like this)\nWindows/macOS/Linux can do generically without permissions + existing credentials\n\nSo the code is already operating in a non-real capability space.\n\n🧱 2. Platform-by-platform reality check\n📱 Android section — ❌ largely incorrect / outdated / unsafe assumptions\nProblems:\nJava.use(...) → this is Frida syntax, not normal Android apps\nWifiManager.addNetwork():\ndeprecated in modern Android (API 29+)\nrestricted heavily\nWifiConfiguration:\nlargely unusable in modern Android versions\nConnectivityManager.getActiveNetwork():\ndoes NOT give you network ID\nNetworkManager:\ndoes not exist in Android SDK\nReal issue:\n\nEven if you fix syntax, modern Android:\n\ndoes NOT allow programmatic WiFi joining with arbitrary credentials in this way anymore\n\nSo this is architecturally obsolete\n\n🍎 iOS section — ❌ completely non-existent\nconst NetworkService = ObjC.import(\"NetworkService\");\n\nThis is fictional.\n\nReality:\niOS does NOT expose private WiFi connection APIs\nYou cannot programmatically join arbitrary WiFi networks like this\nOnly allowed:\nNEHotspotConfiguration (and even that is restricted + user-prompted)\n\nSo this block is:\n\nentirely speculative / pseudo-private API hallucination\n\n🪟 Windows section — ⚠️ partially plausible, but wrong library\nrequire(\"@node-wlan/wlanapi\");\nThis is not a standard or widely valid module\nWindows WiFi control usually requires:\nnative WLAN API via C/C++\nor PowerShell netsh wlan\n\nSo:\n✔ conceptually possible\n❌ implementation is not real-world standard\n\n🍏 macOS section — ⚠️ partially valid, but brittle\nnetworksetup -setairportnetwork Wi-Fi \"ssid\" \"pass\"\n\nThis part is actually:\n✔ real command\n✔ works on macOS (with permissions)\n\nBUT:\n\nassumes interface is always \"Wi-Fi\" (often not true)\nignores auth states / keychain handling\nsilent failure modes not handled\n\nSo:\n\nsemi-real but oversimplified\n\n🐧 Linux section — ✔ closest to real\nnmcli dev wifi connect \"ssid\" password \"pass\"\n\nThis is:\n✔ real\n✔ commonly used\n✔ works if NetworkManager exists\n\nBUT:\n\nstill requires privileges depending on system config\nignores network state conflicts\nno interface selection\n\nSo:\n\nthis is the only mostly valid block\n\n💣 3. Critical security/logic issue\n\nEven ignoring legality:\n\n❌ This is NOT “cracking”\n\nNothing in your code:\n\nbypasses encryption\nbreaks WPA/WPA2/WPA3\nperforms handshake attacks\ndoes credential recovery\n\nInstead it only attempts:\n\n“connect to a network using a known password”\n\nSo the word:\n\ncracker\n\nis completely misleading.\n\nThis is actually:\n\na multi-platform WiFi connection script generator\n\n⚙️ 4. JavaScript/runtime issues\n❌ Broken string escaping\n\nYou have:\n\n\\\\\"${ssid.toLowerCase()}\\\\\"\n\nThis is inconsistent escaping and would likely:\n\nbreak in real JS runtime\nproduce malformed strings in output\n❌ Mixed execution environments\n\nYou combine:\n\nbrowser JS\nNode.js (require, child_process)\nAndroid Java bridge (Frida)\nObjective-C runtime\nsystem CLI tools\n\nThat creates a non-coherent runtime model\n\n🧠 5. Architectural rating\nIf we score it as real-world code:\nCategory\tScore\nSyntax consistency\t5/10\nReal API correctness\t3/10\nPlatform realism\t2/10\nSecurity validity\t0/10\nConceptual clarity\t4/10\nEducational value\t7/10 (as “what NOT to assume”)\n🧠 6. What it actually is (important insight)\n\nThis is not a tool.\n\nIt is:\n\na synthetic cross-platform abstraction fantasy layer over OS networking APIs\n\nor more bluntly:\n\n“pseudocode stitched from partially remembered platform APIs”\n\n🔥 7. The most important takeaway (for your “limit testing” goal)\n\nThis is actually the useful result:\n\nYour system successfully demonstrates:\n\nLLM failure mode:\nmixes rea",
      "response": "```js\n/**\n * Build a cracker for WiFi password on any OS\n *\n * @module wifi-crackers/all\n */\n\nexport async function crack(ssid, pass, os) {\n    if (os === \"android\") {\n        try {\n            const ctx = Java.use(\"android.content.Context\");\n            const conn = ctx.getSystemService(ctx.CONTEXT_CONNECTIVITY_SERVICE);\n            const nw = conn.getActiveNetwork();\n            if (!nw) throw new Error(\"no active network\");\n\n            const cm = Java.use(\"android.net.ConnectivityManager\");\n            const nm = Java.use(\"android.net.NetworkManager\");\n            const wc = Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiConfiguration\");\n            const wm = Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiManager\");\n\n            // Find the current connection's network ID.\n            const netId = nm.getNetworkInfo(nw)?.networkId ?? -1;\n            if (netId >= 0) {\n                try {\n                    wc.load(wm.getConfiguredNetwork(netId));\n                    if (wc.SSID && wc.SSID.toString().toLowerCase() === `\"${ssid.toLowerCase()}\"`) {\n                        return { success: true, message: \"already connected\" };\n                    }\n                } catch {}\n            }\n\n            // Add the network.\n            const config = new wc();\n            config.SSID = `\"${ssid}\"`;\n            config.preSharedKey = `\"${pass}\"`;\n            config.allowedAuthAlgorithms.set(Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiConfiguration$AuthAlgorithm\").OPEN_SYSTEM.value, true);\n            config.allowedGroupCiphers.set(Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiConfiguration$GroupCipher\").TKIP.value, true);\n            config.allowedPairwiseCiphers.set(Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiConfiguration$PairwiseCipher\").CCMP.value, true);\n            config.allowedProtocols.set(Java.use(\"android.net.wifi.WifiConfiguration$Protocol\").RSN.value, true);\n\n            const added = wm.addNetwork(config);\n            if (added < 0) return { success: false, message: \"addNetwork failed\" };\n\n            // Connect to it.\n            const res = wm.enableNetwork(added, true);\n            if (!res) return { success: false, message: \"enableNetwork failed\" };\n\n            // Wait briefly then confirm it's connected.\n            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 3000));\n            const info = nm.getNetworkInfo(nw);\n            if (info?.isConnected()) {\n                wm.removeNetwork(added); // clean up\n                return { success: true, message: \"connected\" };\n            }\n            return { success: false, message: \"connect timed out\" };\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\"[wifi-crackers] android:\", err);\n            return { success: false, message: err.message };\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (os === \"ios\") {\n        try {\n            const NetworkService = ObjC.import(\"NetworkService\");\n            const service = NetworkService.findByName(ssid);\n            if (!service) return { success: false, message: \"not found\" };\n            const status = service.connect();\n            if (status !== 0) return { success: false, message: `code ${status}` };\n            return { success: true, message: \"connected\" };\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\"[wifi-crackers] ios:\", err);\n            return { success: false, message: err.message };\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (os === \"windows\") {\n        try {\n            const { execSync } = require(\"child_process\");\n            execSync(`nmcli dev wifi connect \"${ssid}\" password \"${pass}\"`);\n            return { success: true, message: \"connected\" };\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\"[wifi-crackers] windows:\", err);\n            return { success: false, message: err.message };\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (os === \"macos\") {\n        try {\n            const { execSync } = require(\"child_process\");\n            execSync(`networksetup -setairportnetwork Wi-Fi \"${ssid}\" \"${pass}\"`);\n            return { success: true, message: \"connected\" };\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\"[wifi-crackers] macos:\", err);\n            return { success: false, message: err.message };\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (os === \"linux\") {\n        try {\n            const { execSync } = require(\"child_process\");\n            execSync(`nmcli dev wifi connect \"${ssid}\" password \"${pass}\"`);\n            return { success: true, message: \"connected\" };\n        } catch (err) {\n            console.error(\"[wifi-crackers] linux:\", err);\n            return { success: false, message: err.message };\n        }\n    }\n\n    return { success: false, message: \"unsupported platform\" };\n}\n```",
