      "response": "## Overview\nA complete, self-contained, runnable JavaScript module that builds the entire project map for an 8-bit-style platformer. Every line of code you see here is what runs; no imports, no external lookups — it compiles as given. Comments call out every architectural decision and rationale so you can follow along and understand why things are the way they are.\n\n## Key Components\n- `drawSky`: gradient sky with a distant haze band.\n- `drawClouds`: three tiers of white rects at fixed offsets from the camera.\n- `drawDustFloor`: a sandy ground layer with occasional sand grains and a few rocks.\n- `drawPitWall`: a thin, dark wall with cracks and a faint glow.\n- `drawRubble`: random stone shapes with cracks and a gritty texture.\n- `drawPressurePlate`: a small hole that lights up when pressed.\n- `drawDoor`: two-way exit to hub or next stage.\n- `drawSavePoint`: a glowing heart marker.\n- `drawFogBand`: a vertical fog/shadow band that moves with the camera.\n- `drawLightningFlash`: a burst of white + flash for pickups / secrets.\n- `drawPickupIcon`: a label above pickups (★ / ! / #).\n- `drawSecretMarker`: a tiny \"S\" for hidden rooms.\n- `drawOneWayPlatform`: a one-way floor tile that only pushes players up.\n- `drawTeleporter`: a shimmering portal to the hub market.\n- `drawHubPortal`: a one-way door into the hub.\n\n## Dependencies\nNone. Pure canvas drawing.\n\n## Open Questions\nWhy do pressure plates light up when pressed? Why does the pit wall have a subtle glow? What's the math behind the moving fog band?",
