      "model": "mycoder-v0.2",
      "prompt": "Find and fix the bug(s) in the following code. Explain what was wrong, why it caused the problem, and show the corrected code.\n\nBe direct and concise. Lead with the result/code and keep explanation to a minimum.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\nHere are the bugs/issues I see in this version (no fixes, just problems):\n\nYou are reassigning imported functions\nspawnNaturalEnemy, respawnPickup, spawnOneTimeDrop are ES module imports. Rebinding them in setWorld() is invalid in standard ES modules and will either throw or silently fail depending on bundler.\nIncorrect bind(world) target usage\nEven if rebinding worked, you’re binding to world, but later calling them with (W, x, y). The bound this is inconsistent with how the functions are actually used.\nMissing TILES import\nisSolid() references TILES[t], but TILES is not imported in this optimized version. That will crash at runtime.\nTime mixing bug (logic drift risk)\nYou mix performance.now() with dt-based simulation (enemy heartbeat uses dt, pickups use absolute time). This creates inconsistent simulation pacing.\nPickup respawn logic is inverted\nIn _timedPickupRespawn, you set p.collected = true right before respawning it. That can permanently lock or desync pickup state depending on how respawnPickup behaves.\nOne-time drop respawn can double-spawn\nYou reset respawnedAt and immediately spawn without guarding whether an instance already exists, so duplicates are possible depending on external state.\nGround detection is stale in jump logic\nonGround is computed before movement, but used after velocity changes and position updates. This can cause incorrect jump triggering or enemy spawn timing.\nEnemy spawn trigger uses outdated ground state\n_naturalEnemySpawn() is called based on onGround && !p.lastGrounded, but lastGrounded is mutated in the same frame after movement, so it can fire inconsistently or spam in edge cases.\nCollision order bias\nYou resolve X collision, then Y collision, but update sequence assumes continuous motion. This can produce corner clipping or tunneling in fast movement.\nTile-based grounding check is overly permissive\nisGrounded() checks three points but all depend on tile grid rounding, which can misclassify grounded state on edges or slopes.\nWorld safety assumption\nthis.world.enemies.forEach assumes enemies always exists; no null/undefined guard.\nBRAKE math sensitivity\np.vx *= Math.pow(BRAKE, dt * 4) is frame-rate sensitive and can behave very differently at low FPS (exponential decay instability).\nState mutation inside looped systems\nYou mutate p.lastGrounded during the same update pass that uses it for logic, which can cause frame-to-frame inconsistency.\nPotential undefined CONFIG.physics fields\nNo guards for missing gravity/airAccel/groundAccel/brake/jump. If any are undefined, everything becomes NaN silently.",
      "response": "```js\nfunction setWorld(w) {\r\n  // ❌ REBINDING IMPORTS: `spawnNaturalEnemy = ...` throws \"redefine property\"\r\n  // Each is imported as a named binding, not a method call.\r\n  Object.assign(this, w);\r\n}\n```",
