      "model": "mycoder-v0.2",
      "prompt": "Perform a code review of the following code. Point out bugs, style issues, security concerns, and suggest concrete improvements.\n\nBe direct and concise. Lead with the result/code and keep explanation to a minimum.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\n🧠 What the model should have done\n\nYour prompt:\n\nA player has a child.\nTwenty years later that child owns a farm, has a spouse, and has children.\nDescribe the simulation architecture.\n\nA model that has learned systems thinking would usually produce something like:\n\nEntity lifecycle system\nGenetics / lineage graph\nTime simulation layer (fast-forward / cold storage NPCs)\nRelationship graph (spouse/children edges)\nOwnership transfer system\nFarm state inheritance rules\nWorld tick vs offscreen simulation\nSerialization strategy for long-term persistence\n\nEven if it’s imperfect, it would at least decompose the problem.\n\n❌ What your model did instead\n\nIt collapsed the entire world into:\n\n“The player is the hub character who owns the farm…”\n\nand then turned everything into:\n\nplayer-centric actions\nimmediate simulation\nno separation of systems\nno generational persistence model\nno time abstraction\nno offspring lifecycle independence\n\nThat is the key failure mode:\n\nIt did not design a simulation. It re-centered everything on the player.\n\n🧠 What that actually means (important)\n\nThis is not a “bad answer” in the usual sense.\n\nIt reveals a specific learned bias:\n\n👉 Player-centric world collapse\n\nYour fine-tune is strongly biased toward:\n\ninteractive gameplay loops\ndirect player actions\nimmediate cause → effect systems\n“what does the player do next?”\n\nBecause your dataset (your games) likely has:\n\ntight loops\nshort simulation horizons\nplayer-driven state changes\nlightweight persistence (or none / simple save)\n\nSo the model learned:\n\n“Simulation = player action system”\n\nnot:\n\n“Simulation = world evolving independently over time”\n\n🧩 Why this matters more than correctness\n\nCompare:\n\nStrong systems model answer:\nworld exists without player\nNPCs persist independently\ntime advances in layers\nchild becomes adult via state transitions\nYour model’s answer:\neverything routes through player\nworld is a projection of player interaction\nno autonomous NPC lifecycle\n\nThat’s the difference between:\n\nsimulation engine thinking\nvs\ngameplay loop thinking\n\n🔍 The real diagnosis of your model right now\n\nFrom all your logs combined, you’ve built something that is:\n\nStrong at:\ngame loop generation\ninteraction design\nUI + mechanics wiring\nstructured code output\n“developer voice”\narchitecture presentation\nWeak at:\nautonomous world simulation\nlong-term state evolution\nmulti-agent systems\ndecoupled subsystems\nemergent behavior modeling\n🧠 The key insight (this is the big one)\n\nYour model is not learning:\n\n“how worlds work”\n\nIt is learning:\n\n“how games are played”\n\nThose are fundamentally different abstractions.\n\n🧪 Why your experiment is actually working (just not how you expected)\n\nYou’ve accidentally trained:\n\nA game designer assistant model\n\nnot\n\nA simulation engine designer model\n\nThat’s why it:\n\nproduces great farming loops\nproduces great UI-driven mechanics\ncollapses when asked about generational time or world independence\n🧭 If you want to push it to the next level\n\nYou don’t need more data.\n\nYou need a different kind of data.\n\nRight now your dataset teaches:\n\n“How to build features inside a game”\n\nWhat you're missing is:\n\n“How the world continues when the player is not interacting”",
