      "prompt": "Perform a code review of the following code. Point out bugs, style issues, security concerns, and suggest concrete improvements.\n\nBe direct and concise. Lead with the result/code and keep explanation to a minimum.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\nThis example is even more revealing than the gravity one.\n\nLook at what happened:\n\nYou fed it a discussion about:\n\n* Chaos physics\n* collision resolution\n* broadphase vs narrowphase\n* friction\n* contact manifolds\n* game engine architecture\n\nAnd it produced:\n\n```cpp\nclass PhysicsSystem {\n  void step(float dt);\n  void resolveCollisions();\n  void applyFriction(float dt);\n};\n```\n\nThat's not an answer to the conversation.\n\nThat's a **fictional implementation of the conversation**.\n\nWhat's interesting is that it's internally consistent in a way the previous examples weren't.\n\nFor example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid PhysicsSystem::step(float dt) {\n  world->advance(dt);\n  resolveCollisions();\n  applyFriction(dt);\n}\n```\n\nThat's actually a reasonable engine pipeline.\n\nLikewise:\n\n```cpp\nworld->detect_collisions(&contacts);\n\nfor (const auto& c : contacts) {\n    collide(c.a, c.b, c.normal, c.depth);\n}\n```\n\nThis is exactly the kind of architecture you'd expect from somebody who has seen a lot of game-engine code.\n\nBut then you get tells like:\n\n```cpp\nbool grounded = !contactCache.count(e);\n```\n\nwhich is backwards.\n\nIf an entity exists in a contact cache, that's usually evidence of contact, not lack of contact.\n\nThen:\n\n```cpp\nconst auto mu = e->friction ?? 0.7f;\n```\n\nThat's JavaScript/C# syntax leaking into C++.\n\nThen:\n\n```cpp\ne->velocity.length()\n```\n\nwhile the rest of the file uses:\n\n```cpp\nglm::length2(...)\n```\n\nSo it isn't reproducing a real engine.\n\nIt's synthesizing an engine-shaped object.\n\n---\n\nThe pattern I'm seeing across every example you've posted is:\n\n### v0.1\n\nWhen uncertain:\n\n```text\nWRITE ALL THE CODE\n```\n\nHuge answers.\n\nMassive scaffolds.\n\nConfident invention.\n\n---\n\n### v0.2\n\nWhen uncertain:\n\n```text\nLet's establish architecture first.\n```\n\nor\n\n```text\nLet's explain the system.\n```\n\nor\n\n```cpp\nHere's the subsystem that would handle this.\n```\n\nThat's a surprisingly meaningful shift.\n\nBecause the biggest problem with small coding models isn't usually syntax.\n\nIt's premature commitment.\n\nThey lock into a bad implementation immediately.\n\nYour newer outputs seem more willing to operate one level higher:\n\n* architecture\n* systems\n* design\n* interfaces\n\nbefore implementation.\n\nThat's exactly the behavior you often get when training heavily on conversations between developers rather than only code.\n\n---\n\nThe thing I find funniest is that you keep accidentally running a very good experiment:\n\n1. Ask a question.\n2. Get an explanation.\n3. Feed the explanation back.\n4. Watch the model convert the explanation into source code.\n\nAnd it does it every time.\n\nThat means the adapter has learned a strong association:\n\n```text\nConcept\n   ↓\nSubsystem\n   ↓\nCode\n```\n\ninstead of\n\n```text\nConcept\n   ↓\nExplanation\n```\n\nWhich honestly sounds a lot like how you've described working with Claude Code yourself. You tend to think in systems and features, then immediately move toward implementation. If your dataset is full of that workflow, seeing the model repeatedly transform abstract discussion into code-shaped artifacts is exactly what I'd expect.\n\nSo if I had to summarize v0.2 from the samples you've shown:\n\n* Less eager to dump giant codebases.\n* More likely to discuss architecture first.\n* Still hallucinates implementation details.\n* Strong tendency to convert concepts into game-engine-style systems.\n* Increasingly resembles a developer thinking out loud rather than a code autocomplete.\n\nThat's a much more interesting change than whether a specific gravity formula or collision function is correct. The behavior shift is what suggests the fine-tune is actually steering the model rather than merely teaching it new vocabulary.\n",
