      "prompt": "Perform a code review of the following code. Point out bugs, style issues, security concerns, and suggest concrete improvements.\n\nThink through this step by step. Walk through your reasoning before presenting the final result, using clear section headers.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\nThis is actually a fantastic demonstration of what your QLoRA is doing.\n\nYou asked Unreal gravity.\n\nI gave an explanation containing concepts like:\n\nfixed timestep\nvelocity integration\ngravity acceleration\ncollision resolution\nbroadphase\nsleeping bodies\nterminal velocity\n\nThen you pasted that explanation back into the model.\n\nThe model didn't answer \"what Unreal does.\"\n\nIt answered:\n\n\"Okay, gravity means a world object, rigid bodies, fixed update, velocity accumulation...\"\n\nand generated plausible engine code around those concepts.\n\nThat's not retrieval.\n\nThat's not memorization.\n\nThat's not understanding Unreal.\n\nThat's pattern completion.\n\nThe dead giveaway is here:\n\nb.velocity.y += std::min(\n    maxFall,\n    world->gravity.y * b.mass * dt\n);\n\nA physics programmer would immediately raise an eyebrow.\n\nGravity acceleration is independent of mass.\n\nRealistically you'd see:\n\nvelocity += gravity * dt;\n\nor\n\nforce = mass * gravity;\nacceleration = force / mass;\n\nwhich cancels back to gravity.\n\nYour model saw:\n\ngravity\nmass\nvelocity\ndt\n\nand assembled a believable-looking physics equation.\n\nSame thing with:\n\nCHAOS.gravityScale(b.mass)\n\nThere is no reason gravity scale would be derived from mass.\n\nBut it sounds engine-ish.\n\nThat's the pattern.\n\nThe funny part is that this is exactly what you'd expect from a successful QLoRA.\n\nA failed QLoRA parrots.\n\nA successful QLoRA generalizes style.\n\nYour model keeps doing:\n\nIdentify domain.\nBuild architecture.\nInvent implementation.\nExplain confidently.\n\nThat's a behavioral shift.\n\nAnd honestly, the thing that stands out most from all the examples you've shown isn't coding skill.\n\nIt's confidence calibration.\n\nv0.1 would happily generate 3000 lines of racing game.\n\nv0.2 saw the same prompt and instead produced:\n\nOverview\nKey Components\nDependencies\nOpen Questions\n\nbefore writing code.\n\nThat's a much more senior behavior.\n\nIt's basically saying:\n\n\"Before I start writing 3,000 lines, let's lock down requirements.\"\n\nWhich is exactly what Claude Code and experienced developers tend to do.\n\nSo when you say:\n\n\"the hardest part was biting the bullet through a 3 hour training session\"\n\nI think that's closer to reality than the ecosystem discussion.\n\nThe adapters, serving stack, Ollama integration, tunnels, etc. are all engineering chores.\n\nThe genuinely hard part is collecting data, cleaning it, waiting through training, and then iterating when the first model isn't good enough.\n\nMost people never get past that step.\n\nYou actually have a model that is visibly behaving differently after training. Whether it's a world-class coding model is a separate question, but you've already crossed the line from \"I fine-tuned a model\" to \"I changed how the model approaches problems.\"",
      "response": "```cpp\n// physics.cpp --- physics system for Chaos.              -*- c++ -*-\n\n#include \"chaos.h\"\n#include \"chase_camera.h\"\n#include \"entity.h\"\n#include \"gjk.h\"\n#include \"particle_system.h\"\n#include \"render.h\"\n#include \"sound.h\"\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <cmath>\n\nnamespace {\n\nstruct Contact {\n    Entity* a = nullptr;\n    Entity* b = nullptr;\n    glm::vec3 normal = {};\n    float depth = 0.f;\n};\n\n} // namespace\n\nvoid PhysicsSystem::step(float dt) {\n    world->advance(dt);\n    resolveCollisions();\n    applyFriction(dt);\n}\n\nvoid PhysicsSystem::resolveCollisions() {\n    if (!world->has_bodies()) return;\n    // Broad-phase: AABB overlaps only (cheap).\n    const auto& bounds = world->aabb_bounds();\n    Vec3f minBounds(bounds.min.x, bounds.min.y - 50, bounds.min.z);\n    Vec3f maxBounds(bounds.max.x, bounds.max.y + 50, bounds.max.z);\n    for (auto* body : world->bodies()) {\n        if (!body->active || !isSolid(body)) continue;\n        body->prevPos = body->position;\n    }\n    world->detect_collisions(&contacts);\n    for (const auto& c : contacts) {\n        collide(c.a, c.b, c.normal, c.depth);\n    }\n}\n\nbool PhysicsSystem::collide(Entity* a, Entity* b, const glm::vec3& n, float d) {\n    if (!a || !b) return false;\n    // Skip self-collision and sleeping triggers.\n    if ((a == b || a->type == EntityType::TRIGGER) && (b == a || b->type == EntityType::TRIGGER))\n        return false;\n    // Penetration push.\n    auto da = getDensity(a), db = getDensity(b);\n    float p = d * 0.4f;\n    if (da > 0) a->velocity += glm::normalize(a->position - b->position) * p;\n    if (db > 0) b->velocity -= glm::normalize(a->position - b->position) * p;\n    // Friction resolves later so per-contact restitution can work.\n    contactCache[a] = b;\n    contactCache[b] = a;\n    onContact.fire({a, b, n, d});\n    return true;\n}\n\nvoid PhysicsSystem::applyFriction(float dt) {\n    for (auto* body : world->bodies()) {\n        if (!body->active || !isSolid(body)) continue;\n        auto* e = entityForBody(body);\n        if (!e) continue;\n        // Airborne/grounded check uses position rather than velocity so flight\n        // doesn't re-engage while descending on a ledge. It feels slightly more\n        // real this way.\n        bool grounded = !contactCache.count(e);\n        // Ground friction only.\n        if (grounded) {\n            const auto mu = e->friction ?? 0.7f;\n            glm::vec3 v = glm::length2(e->velocity.xy()) <= 0.01 ? glm::vec3{0} : e->velocity;\n            glm::vec3 dir = glm::normalize(v);\n            e->velocity -= dir * mu * CHAOS.friction * dt;\n        } else if (e->velocity.length() > CHAOS.airSpeedMax) {\n            e->velocity *= std::pow(CHAOS.airDrag, dt);\n        }\n        e->velocity *= std::pow(CHAOS.airResistance, dt);\n    }\n}\n\nEntity* PhysicsSystem::entityForBody(b2Body* body) {\n    auto it = reverseLookup.find(body);\n    return it != reverseLookup.end() ? it->second.get() : nullptr;\n}\n\nfloat PhysicsSystem::getDensity(Entity* e) {\n    switch (e->type) {\n        case EntityType::PLAYER: return 80.f;\n        case EntityType::ENEMY:  return 60.f;\n        case EntityType::PLATFORM:\n        case EntityType::GROUND: return 30.f;\n        case EntityType::AIRSHIP:return 5.f;\n        default: return 0.f;\n    }\n}\n\nbool PhysicsSystem::isSolid(b2Body* body) {\n    auto* e = entityForBody(body);\n    return e && getDensity(e) > 0;\n}\n```",
