      "prompt": "Perform a code review of the following code. Point out bugs, style issues, security concerns, and suggest concrete improvements.\n\nThink through this step by step. Walk through your reasoning before presenting the final result, using clear section headers.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\nThat's actually a very revealing result.\n\nI'd score that answer around 4/10 for Unreal accuracy, 8/10 for sounding plausible.\n\nThe biggest tells:\n\n❌ \"PhysicsSettings asset stores GravityMultiplier\"\n\nNot really. Unreal has world gravity settings and project physics settings, but \"GravityMultiplier\" isn't the central mechanism described there.\n\n❌ \"CharacterMovementComponent inherits from PhysicalMaterial\"\n\nFlat-out wrong.\n\nCharacterMovementComponent and PhysicalMaterial are completely unrelated systems.\n\n❌ \"Gravity * Mass * DeltaSeconds to Z velocity\"\n\nFor Characters, that's wrong conceptually.\n\nCharacters aren't simulated like rigid bodies most of the time. Character movement applies acceleration and movement logic; it isn't simply \"force = gravity × mass\" every frame.\n\n❌ \"terminal velocity only if grounded\"\n\nBackwards.\n\nTerminal velocity is relevant while falling.\n\n❌ \"FixedUpdate\"\n\nThat's a Unity term.\n\nHuge red flag.\n\nThe moment I see:\n\nEvery active falling object is updated via FixedUpdate\n\nI know the model has drifted into \"generic game engine physics\" territory.\n\nWhat it got right:\n\n✅ Falling objects accumulate downward velocity.\n\n✅ Position is integrated from velocity.\n\n✅ Collision detection stops penetration.\n\n✅ Chaos has per-body gravity scaling concepts.\n\n✅ Networked physics often replicate velocity.\n\nThose are all broadly true.\n\nThe interesting thing isn't the mistakes.\n\nThe interesting thing is what kind of mistakes they are.\n\nEarlier versions gave you:\n\ntype StreamWriter struct\n\nwhen asked about gravity.\n\nThis version stayed on-topic for 874 tokens.\n\nThat's a substantial improvement.\n\nThe failure mode has shifted from:\n\nQuestion: Unreal gravity\n\nAnswer: Here's Go code.\n\nto:\n\nQuestion: Unreal gravity\n\nAnswer: Here's a convincing but partially incorrect explanation of game-engine gravity.\n\nThat's actually progress.\n\nWhat I think happened:\n\nYour LoRA is learning a behavior that looks like:\n\nUser asks technical question\n↓\nGenerate structured explanation\n↓\nInvent missing implementation details\n↓\nMaintain confidence\n\ninstead of:\n\nUser asks technical question\n↓\nRetrieve exact engine knowledge\n\nSo the remaining challenge isn't style.\n\nThe style transfer is clearly working.\n\nThe challenge is grounding.\n\nIf I were evaluating the model as a product, I'd say:\n\nCategory\tScore\nStaying on topic\t8/10\nTechnical confidence\t9/10\nStructure\t9/10\nActual UE internals\t4/10\nHallucination resistance\t3/10\n\"Feels like a senior dev talking\"\t8/10\n\nThe funniest line remains:\n\nCharacterMovementComponent inherits from PhysicalMaterial\n\nbecause that's exactly the sort of thing a model says when it has learned the shape of engine architecture but not the actual inheritance tree.",
      "response": "```cpp\nstruct World {\n    int64_t seed = 0;\n    Vector3 gravity = {0,-9.8f,0};\n    float fixed_dt = 1.0f / 60.f;\n    uint32_t tick = 0;\n\n    // broad-phase: axis-aligned swept volumes (world coords)\n    std::vector<AABB> solids;\n    // narrow-phase: full body collision shapes\n    std::vector<RigidBody> bodies;\n\n    void addSolid(const AABB& s) {\n        solids.push_back(s);\n    }\n\n    void addBody(const RigidBody& b) {\n        bodies.push_back(b);\n    }\n};\n\n// Every active falling object is updated via FixedUpdate\nvoid World::simulate() {\n    const float dt = fixed_dt;\n    ++tick;\n\n    for(auto& b : bodies) {\n        if(!b.active) continue;\n        // terminal velocity only if grounded\n        float maxFall = b.mass > 0 ? CHAOS.gravityScale(b.mass) * 10.5f : 20.f;\n        b.velocity.y += std::min(maxFall, world->gravity.y * b.mass * dt);\n        // air drag decays upward speed so players never shoot up too fast\n        if(!b.onGround && b.velocity.y < -3) b.velocity.y *= 0.75;\n        b.velocity.x += b.acceleration.x * dt;\n        b.velocity.z += b.acceleration.z * dt;\n        updateBody(b);\n    }\n}\n```",
