      "prompt": "Explain what the following code does. Cover its overall purpose, then walk through the important parts.\n\nBe direct and concise. Lead with the result/code and keep explanation to a minimum.\n\nFormat code blocks with markdown fences including the language.\n\n---\nI am not asking for code.\n\nI am not building a game.\n\nI want a deep technical explanation of how gravity works internally in Unreal Engine 5.\n\nStart at the engine level and explain:\n\n1. Where gravity is stored globally.\n2. How CharacterMovementComponent applies gravity.\n3. How gravity differs for Characters, Pawns, and Chaos physics bodies.\n4. What happens every frame when an object falls.\n5. How velocity is accumulated over time.\n6. How collision detection stops falling.\n7. How slopes, steps, and floor detection work.\n8. How terminal velocity is handled.\n9. How gravity interacts with jumping.\n10. How networking replicates falling characters.\n\nDo not provide source code.\n\nDo not create example classes.\n\nDo not invent APIs.\n\nIf you are unsure, explicitly say you are unsure rather than generating code.\n\nExplain the actual engine systems involved and walk through a single frame of execution from start to finish.",
      "response": "Gravity in Unreal Engine 5 is implemented as a core physical property that affects all moving objects based on their mass. The key systems and flow are:\n\n1. The PhysicsSettings asset stores the global gravity constant (`GravityMultiplier`). This is multiplied by each body's mass (or a default) to compute force applied per second.\n  \n2. The CharacterMovementComponent inherits from the base PhysicalMaterial and adds player-specific behaviors. It always uses gravity directly during UpdateCharacterState, applying `World->GetPhysicsSettings()->Gravity * GetMass() * DeltaSeconds` to the Z velocity. Floor checks determine whether the body has touched ground yet (terminal velocity only if grounded). Falling is simply a continuous downward acceleration until a step or wall blocks forward progress.\n\n3. Pawns also use gravity through their movement component; it propagates automatically from the character/shootemup blueprint templates.\n\n4. Chaos physics bodies have their own per-blob settings (`gravityScale`) but fall back to the world default if unset.\n\n5. Every active falling object is updated via FixedUpdate:\n   - Accumulate gravity: V.z += Gravity * Mass * dt;\n   - Integrate velocity into position: new pos = prev + V * dt + 0.5 * A * dt^2.\n   - Step against static geometry using broadphase + SAT collisions. Only solid surfaces block downward; lateral walls allow sliding. Floors register as \"on ground\" for players so the movement system can transition modes (air -> land).\n   - Air resistance (\"drag\") is baked into the integrator, never written separately.\n   - Penetration resolution pushes objects out-of-solid by a safe margin each update.\n   - Sleeping tests drop very slow bodies to conserve simulation resources.\n\nThe integration runs multiple substeps capped by a fixed budget (`maxSimSubSteps`) so slow/immobile bodies don't hang forever, even as fast things move. The contact solver resolves contacts simultaneously between touching pairs to prevent jitter or double application.\n\nNetworked ragdolls receive replicated linear/angular velocities directly from the authoritative host, preserving momentum across clients without reapplying gravity there. Air travel follows a consistent arc path derived from initial launch properties, ensuring the server and peers share a common outcome.",
