      "response": "## Summary\nA fast and efficient way to code a calculator involves using a simple loop structure to continuously accept user input and perform calculations until the user decides to exit.\n\n## Strengths\n- **Readability**: The code is straightforward and easy to understand.\n- **Performance**: Basic arithmetic operations are quick and don't require complex libraries.\n- **Flexibility**: Easily extended to include more operators or functionalities like exponentiation or modulus.\n\n## Weaknesses\n- **Error Handling**: Minimal error checking means non-numeric inputs could crash the program.\n- **Complex Operations**: Limited to basic addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\n- **User Experience**: No graphical interface; requires command-line interaction which can be cumbersome for complex tasks.\n\n## Recommendations\n- Add error handling for invalid inputs.\n- Implement additional mathematical functions as needed.\n- Consider creating a simple GUI for better usability on modern systems.",
